-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- RFC-0021 — One availability switch
--
-- Delta only. Everything not mentioned here stays as earlier RFCs defined it.
--
-- TWO EDITS AT ORIGIN, listed here for the record and made in the files that
-- own them (no ALTER — nothing is deployed and rfc/*.schema applied in order
-- IS the schema):
--
--   1. RFC-0002 · availability_status loses three of its four values.
--   2. RFC-0018 · user_work_preferences gains open_to_freelance.
--
-- The two are one change seen from both ends. Freelance stops being a state of
-- LOOKING and becomes a shape of WORK, which is where every other kind of
-- engagement already lives.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1 · availability_status, at origin in RFC-0002
--
--     CREATE TYPE availability_status AS ENUM ('not_looking', 'looking');
--
-- It had four values: not_looking, looking_for_job, looking_for_freelance and
-- open_to_freelance. Three of them said the same thing — "yes" — and differed
-- only in what KIND of work the person wanted, which the OpenTo* flags already
-- answer and answer better, because those compose and an enum makes somebody
-- choose.
--
-- The cost of the four was not the extra values, it was the overlap: a
-- contributor could tick 'contract' and set 'looking_for_freelance', and
-- nothing could say which of the two the platform should believe. ADR-0018 §2
-- refused an open_to_freelance FLAG for exactly that reason — two controls
-- meaning one thing can disagree. Removing the duplication from the STATUS
-- instead resolves it the other way round, and better: what work somebody
-- wants is now said in one place, in the vocabulary the roles already use.
--
-- WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, and is the reason this stays an enum rather than
-- becoming a boolean:
--
--   * 'not_looking' is an OPT-OUT that no toggle reveals (ADR-0008 §1a). It
--     has to be distinguishable from a row that simply lapsed, and from no row
--     at all — three states, one column;
--   * expires_at stays, and so does the 15-day window (ADR-0010 §5). It is
--     what stops the pool filling with people who stopped looking a year ago,
--     and nothing about collapsing the values touches it.


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2 · user_work_preferences.open_to_freelance, at origin in RFC-0018
--
--     open_to_freelance boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false,
--
-- The fifth shape, alongside remote, onsite, contract and internship. Default
-- false like the others: a profile nobody filled in surfaces nobody, and
-- opting somebody into freelance work by inaction would put them in front of
-- hirers they never agreed to hear from.
--
-- ADR-0018 §2 explicitly refused this flag. The reason it gave was sound and
-- is now spent: availability_status carried freelance twice over, so a flag
-- would have been a third place to say it. Availability no longer says it at
-- all, so the flag is the only place — which is what the rule was protecting.


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3 · Nothing else.
--
-- No table is created here. A schema file that adds nothing is unusual enough
-- to say why: this RFC is two edits at origin and a large amount of meaning,
-- and putting the columns anywhere but the tables that own them would split
-- one contributor's profile across two files for no reason.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
